{"id": "31261", "revid": "2063662", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=31261", "title": "San Mateo (California)", "text": "San Mateo is a town on the Peninsula in the Bay Area of California.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nSan Mateo is near ('). The Bay Area's other airports, (') and (), are less convenient.\nBy rail.\nThe CalTrain calls at three stations in San Mateo:, and.\nBy car.\nUS-101 and I-280 run through the city. It is also connected to the East Bay via the San Mateo\u2013Hayward Bay Bridge ($7 toll as of 2022).\nGet around.\nThe most convenient way is by car. If you're using public transit, check out the trip planner on http://www.511.org.\nCycling and walking are also popular ways of getting around. There are two downtown shopping areas: the primary downtown is between 2nd and 5th streets, bordered by El Camino Real and the train tracks. The big, new San Mateo Public Library is also downtown. The \"other\" downtown is south about 15 blocks, at 25th ave on the West side of El Camino Real. Continuing south on El Camino Real is the Hillsdale Mall.\nSee.\nCentral Park is off of El Camino Real between 5th Ave and 9th Ave. Inside the park is a beautiful. There is also a for children that runs daily in summer and at weekends all year. During the winter, there is an outside ice rink that is open to the public daily (including holidays).\nNorth of the park, downtown San Mateo runs along 3rd Avenue and 4th Avenue (spilling somewhat onto 2nd Avenue) between El Camino Real and the Caltrain train tracks; it's one of the more successful urban downtowns left on the Peninsula. The streets with the most restaurants and other establishments are B Street, 3rd Avenue, 4th Avenue and 2nd Avenue.\nSan Mateo used to be home to Bay Meadows, the only horse racing track on the San Francisco Peninsula. It's being redeveloped. The grounds are also home to the San Mateo County Events Center, where the annual county fair is held and various other events (including the 2010 Bay Area stop of Maker Faire. Take the Delaware Street exit off of Highway 92 and head south; the Event Center parking lot is directly off Delaware. What part of Bay Meadows remains visible are best viewed on foot from this area.\nFor fans of the local mid-century modern architecture, San Mateo is home to two Eichler neighborhoods - 19th Avenue park is a very early tract group of Eichlers, and The Highlands is a later neighborhood of custom Eichlers.\nCoyote Point is a park in the north-east corner of San Mateo off of Highway 101 at Peninsula Avenue. A small boat harbor is there as well as a natural history museum. There is also a public shooting range, open weekday evenings.\nPoplar Creek Golf Course, a municipal course, is there as well. A hiking trail allows easy access to San Francisco Bay.\nCrystal Springs Reservoir is in the watershed area off of Highway 280 on the western edge of San Mateo. Crystal Springs dam is just south of Crystal Springs off of Skyline Boulevard.\nDo.\nAlong with shopping and dining, there is a medium-size movie theater (12 screens, none of them huge) just off B-street between 3rd and 2nd Avenues.\nDrink.\nMany of the restaurants in downtown have full bars - good for a pre- or post-movie drink, but many close relatively early and there is no urban nightlife scene to speak of. There are a few local bars (, another on Pacific a few blocks south of Hillsdale) and a small nightclub at the Crowne Plaza Hotel in neighboring Foster City."}
{"id": "31263", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=31263", "title": "Sanmenxia", "text": "Sanmenxia (\u4e09\u95e8\u5ce1; S\u0101nm\u00e9nxi\u00e1) is a city in western Henan with 2.2 million inhabitants. Situated along the upper reaches of the Yellow River, Sanmenxia is known for its breathtaking natural scenery and historical significance. The city is home to the impressive Sanmenxia Dam, a major engineering landmark, and boasts a rich cultural heritage with ancient sites and scenic spots, making it a popular destination for both history enthusiasts and nature lovers. \nUnderstand.\nSanmenxia is on the southern bank of the Yellow River. Sanmenxia city is one of the cradles of Chinese civilization. During the Warring States period, this city guarded the strategic pass between the Qin forces in the Wei Valley to the west and the kingdoms of the North China Plain to the east. It is here that Laozi wrote the Dao De Jing, the founding charter and source of Taoism.\nIn 1921, the first of late Stone Age paintings were found in MianChiXian Yangshao village. These paintings showed a Chinese matriarchal society. The patterned painted pottery is one of the important features of what came to be known at Yanghao culture.\nAccording to legend, when Dayu was controlling floods, he swung his magic axe to split the mountains into three canyons, namely \"Human Gate\", \"God Gate\" and \"Ghost Gate\", and led the water of the Yellow River to flow eastward, hence the name Sanmenxia. Sanmenxia City is known as \"Five Mountains, Four Ridges and One River\". At the same time, Yangshao Culture, Taoist Culture and Guo State Culture all originated here.\nToday, the city is mostly known for the dam of the same name, built in revolutionary fervor with the semi-mythical goal of clearing the Yellow River. Tourists come during the rainy season to witness massive discharges of the river's water.\nHistory.\nLegend has it that Yu the Great, while controlling floods, split the mountains into three gorges\u2014Human Gate, God Gate, and Ghost Gate\u2014allowing the Yellow River to flow eastward. This gave Sanmenxia its name. Located at the foot of the Xiaoshan Mountains, part of the Qinling Range, the region includes the historic Hangu Pass, also known as \"Xiaohan.\"\nSome 6,000 years ago, large tribes settled here. Between the 21st and 13th centuries BCE, it was the core of the Xia and Shang dynasties. During the Western Zhou, it belonged to the Jiao and Guo states; later, in the Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods, it was divided among Han, Qin, and Wei. Under the Qin and Han dynasties, it became part of Sanchuan and Hongnong commanderies, respectively.\nIn the Republic of China period, it was part of Henan\u2019s administrative districts. In 1944, an anti-Japanese base was established in western Henan. After the founding of the People\u2019s Republic, Shan Prefecture was established, and Sanmenxia became a prefecture-level city in 1986.\nClimate.\nSanmenxia has a warm temperate continental monsoon climate with semi-arid characteristics. The city experiences distinct four seasons, with an average annual temperature of 14.2\u00b0C and total annual precipitation ranging from 400 to 700 millimeters. Summers can be hot, with temperatures often reaching 30\u00b0C, while winters are cold and dry, with temperatures that can drop below \u221210\u00b0C and occasional snowfall. The best time to visit is in spring and autumn when the weather is pleasant, and the scenery is most vibrant.\nGet in.\nSanmenxia is a city with convenient transportation. It is connected to Guanzhong in the west, Shanxi in the north, and guards the Central Plains in the east. The Longhai Railway, Lianhuo Expressway, National Highway 310, and Zhengxi High-speed Railway run across the east and west, and National Highway 209, San (Menxia) Xi (Chuan) Expressway and Haoji Railway connect the north and the south. It is a regional transportation hub city connecting the three provinces of Henan, Shanxi and Shaanxi, and going north and south, west and east. \nBy plane.\nSanmenxia has no civil airport. The nearest airports are Luoyang Beijiao Airport, about 150 km away, and Xi'an Xianyang International Airport, about 240 km away. You can take the high-speed rail or bus to Sanmenxia.\nBy rail.\nSanmenxia has two railway stations: Sanmenxia Railway Station and Sanmenxianan Railway Station. High-speed rail lines reach major cities such as Beijing, Xi'an, and Zhengzhou, while ordinary trains connect surrounding cities.\nBy bus.\nLianhuo Expressway and Erguang Expressway run through Sanmenxia. Long-distance buses are a convenient choice from Luoyang, Xi'an and other places.\nBy car.\nSelf-driving is a good choice. Lianhuo Expressway and Erguang Expressway provide convenient traffic conditions.Traveling to Sanmenxia from Xi'an by car takes approximately 3 hours, while the journey from Luoyang is about 2 hours.\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nBuses are a cheap and convenient way to travel within Sanmenxia, costing only \u00a51 per ride. Routes can be tricky to figure out, so using local maps or asking for assistance is recommended. Bus No. 2, for instance, runs from the train station to Shanzhou Park, making it a useful route for visitors.\nBy taxi.\nTaxis in Sanmenxia are an easy way to get around, with a starting fare of \u00a58 for the first 2 kilometers. After 1.5 kilometers, the fare increases by \u00a51.8 per kilometer, with no additional fuel surcharges. Taxis are particularly convenient for trips between districts or areas not well-connected by buses.\nCity layout and distances.\nSanmenxia covers a large geographic area, with significant distances between its districts and counties:\nThese long distances make planning ahead essential, especially for inter-district travel.\nTravel tips.\nInter-district travel: For trips between urban and rural areas, long-distance buses or private vehicles are the primary options. Travel times between districts can range from 30 minutes to over 2 hours, depending on the distance and mode of transport.\nFor tourists: Ride-hailing apps like Didi Chuxing are widely available and helpful for planning trips both within the city and to outlying districts.\nEat.\nOne-of-a-kind restaurants\nWestern chains\nConnect.\nThere is free wifi everywhere you go here."}
{"id": "31267", "revid": "36420", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=31267", "title": "San Miguel", "text": "There is more than one place called San Miguel:\nPhilippines.\nThe Philippines has about a dozen towns and islands named San Miguel; see Wikipedia for a list. None of these are large, and none have Wikivoyage articles as of mid-2020.\nThe country's largest brewery is named San Miguel and their beer is ubiquitous."}
